  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA more active weather pattern is in store for the Tri-State area \nnext week. Synoptically, we begin with a ridge centered over the \nupper Mississippi Valley on Tuesday. This system looks to be \npositively tilted as a longwave trough moves across the western \nCONUS and pushes said ridge eastward. Southwest flow aloft should \nresult as our county warning area (CWA) sits right in between the \ntwo Rossby waves. On Wednesday, the aforementioned trough \ndeamplifies quite a bit, sending a fairly strong upper-level jet \nthrough our region. Guidance is a little more uncertain after this \nprocess but most ensembles have weak ridging that forms Thursday \nbefore another trough moves onshore over the Pacific Northwest. This \nnext troughing pattern brings with it another closed low aloft late \nnext week that will define conditions for our area, dependent on the \ntrack and strength of the system overall. Models are still in \ndisagreement on these two parameters at the moment.\n\nThere will be two opportunities for rain during this four-day \nperiod. The first of which presents via a surface low that is \nforecasted to move north of the CWA on Wednesday with trough #1. \nThere should be a cold front with this system but guidance currently \nhas dew points in the 30s after it passes so any precipitation would \nbe pre-frontal. At the moment, there is low confidence of rain with \nthis first system as Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) stand at \naround 10-20%. Trough #2 should be accompanied by another low-\npressure system on Friday that could bring another front through the \nTri-State area. Confidence on rainfall is surprisingly high given \nthe uncertain track of the low. PoPs are currently 50-70% throughout \nthe entire CWA but trends regarding evolution of this system will \nneed to be monitored heading into next week.\n\nAnother potential result from this upbeat synoptic pattern is \nboosted surface winds. Two different upper-level jets are \nforecasted to influence our area, ushering in breezy conditions\nat the surface. As the first jet passes overhead Tuesday, 20-35\nmph southerly winds are likely. Gusts up to 40 mph will be \npossible too, especially in eastern parts of the CWA. \nWednesday's winds should be just as strong, but with the \nstronger gusts located in the Colorado counties as well as east \nof US-83. There is a low chance(20%) of gusts up to 45 mph on \nWednesday. Winds die down Thursday after the first system \ndeparts but 10-20 mph is still in the realm of possibilities. \nFriday's system should bring more windy conditions with a \ngeneral 20- 30 anticipated. Gusts in the 45-50 mph will be \npossible in eastern Colorado.\n\nTemperatures are likely to be average or even slightly above average \nfor this time of the year. Highs in the mid to upper 50s Tuesday \nshould warm into 60s Wednesday. Thursdays high temps sit in the 52-\n60 degree range and Friday shows consistent 60s as well. Nighttime \nlows will start warmer on Wednesday in the 35-42 range but will fall \nquite a bit after the first cold front Thursday morning with 30s \nexpected across the board. Some locations in the western CWA may \ndrop into the 20s and other locations east of Colby may only fall \ninto the upper 30s. Nevertheless, Friday morning low should bounce \nback a bit into the upper 30s/low 40s. Fire weather should remain a \nnon-factor for the time being with RHs generally above 40%. Fog may \nbe possible with RHs reaching 100% Wednesday and Friday\nmornings.",
